---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import AvifToPng from '@/components/tools/image-convert/AvifToPng.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="avif-to-png">
  <AvifToPng client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert AVIF to PNG</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop the .avif files onto the box above.</li>
      <li>Conversion starts immediately. PNG is lossless, so there is no quality setting.</li>
      <li>Transparency is kept automatically. Turn on the flatten option only if you want a solid colour behind the artwork.</li>
      <li>Save each PNG, or download all of them as one ZIP.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The usual reason: getting AVIF into an editor</h2>
    <p>
      Creative software does not support AVIF well yet. Many image editors need a plug-in or a recent version,
      most stock-photo and print-on-demand uploaders reject it, and
      much internal company software does not know it. PNG is lossless, supports transparency, and
      almost all software can read it.
    </p>

    <h2>What survives the conversion</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th align="left">Property</th>
            <th align="left">Result in the PNG</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Pixel values</td><td>Reproduced exactly as decoded; PNG adds no loss</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Alpha channel</td><td>Kept at full 8-bit precision</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Existing AVIF artefacts</td><td>Copied exactly; they are now part of the image</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10-bit / HDR data</td><td>Converted down to 8-bit sRGB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Animation (AVIS sequences)</td><td>First frame only</td></tr>
          <tr><td>EXIF, XMP, ICC profile</td><td>Not carried over; output is plain sRGB</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Be ready for a much larger file</h2>
    <p>
      AVIF is one of the most efficient lossy image codecs; PNG cannot discard anything. The file gets
      much larger, and this is normal:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A 180 KB AVIF photograph usually becomes a 3–6 MB PNG.</li>
      <li>A flat vector-style illustration can only double, because PNG handles large areas of one colour well.</li>
      <li>Screenshots and UI captures often grow very little, for the same reason.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For a copy to view and share that does not need to be lossless, <a href="/avif-to-jpg">AVIF to JPG</a>
      gives a much smaller file. For transparency and a small file, convert to WebP with the
      <a href="/image-converter">universal image converter</a>. To convert in the other direction, use
      <a href="/image-to-avif">image to AVIF</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
